#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# conditional_expression

#% text
# `conditional_expression(value, condition)` is a value that holds only where `condition` holds.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# With the condition `true` the result is the value; with `false` it is `undefined`.

#% item
# Arithmetic, comparisons, `and`, `or`, `not` and the elementary and special functions take the condition outward: `f(conditional_expression(a, c), b)` is `conditional_expression(f(a, b), c)`, and the conditions of several arguments are joined by `and`.

#% item
# A joined condition is simplified once: parts implied by the others are dropped, contradictory parts make the result `undefined`, and bounds on one real variable are written as intervals.

#% item
# `simplify`, `refine`, `expand`, `factor`, `d`, `integrate`, `limit` and `n` work on the value under the condition and keep the condition. `normal` gives the value, and `reduce` adds the condition to the equations.

#% item
# Lists keep conditional elements: a list of conditional values is not one conditional value.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A value that holds for positive x:

#% cell
conditional_expression(x^2, x > 0)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(x^2, x > 0)

#% text
# True and false conditions:

#% cell
[conditional_expression(a, true), conditional_expression(a, false)]
#% output
#= [a, undefined]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Functions take the conditions outward and join them:

#% cell
sin(conditional_expression(x, x > 0) + 1)*conditional_expression(x, x < 1)^2
#% output
#= conditional_expression(x^2*sin(1 + x), 0 < x and x < 1)

#% text
# Contradictory conditions give `undefined`:

#% cell
conditional_expression(x, x > 0) + conditional_expression(x, x < 0)
#% output
#= undefined

#% text
# Comparisons and logical connectives:

#% cell
conditional_expression(p, c) and (conditional_expression(q, d) or r)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(p and (q or r), c and d)

#% text
# `simplify` uses the condition:

#% cell
simplify(conditional_expression(sqrt(x^2), x < 0))
#% output
#= conditional_expression(-x, x < 0)

#% text
# Calculus works on the value:

#% cell
integrate(conditional_expression(a^x, a > 0), x)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(a^x/log(a), a > 0)

#% cell
limit(conditional_expression(a^x, 0 < a and a < 1), x -> inf)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(0, 0 < a and a < 1)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `normal` drops the condition:

#% cell
normal(conditional_expression(x^2, x > 0))
#% output
#= x^2

#% text
# `reduce` adds the condition to the equation:

#% cell
reduce(conditional_expression(x^2 - 1, x > 0) == 0, x)
#% output
#= x == 1

#% text
# `refine` refines each value under its own condition:

#% cell
refine([conditional_expression(sqrt(x^2), x > 0), conditional_expression(sqrt(x^2), x < 0)])
#% output
#= [conditional_expression(x, x > 0), conditional_expression(-x, x < 0)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `assuming`, `refine`, `simplify`, `reduce`, `piecewise`
